# Sitting r0928, job `gt` (item 5; plan-triage.md ⑪ in the flowg line, "먼저 scratchpad/cardinread/gtdram.card
# ncu ... 재독이면 3.41 GB, L2가 잡으면 0.72 GB"; cardinread's draft card, whose band and decisions this
# card keeps). The prose tile set of a full-block layer, about 9.4 MB of q8_1 columns and 1.9 MB of
# weight rows a sweep, is past the 6 MB L2, so a GT sweep per 8-row tile re-reads its activations:
# 3.41 GB a launch [derived, cardinread]; held in L2, 0.72 GB. The draft's layer-10 launch is taken here
# as the median over the call's full-block GT launches (grid within 2 % of the largest), since the
# runner cannot name a layer without the nsys plan and the tile grids of the MoE layers of one prose
# prompt are the same shape (tile_cap counts host slots too, triage ⑩, so the grid is the tile cap's).
# Minutes: 5 [the spec's estimate; one V4.1 load 75-117 s, ~86 profiled launches in one pass].
kind: profile
question: does the tiled gate-up re-read its tiles' activations from DRAM once per 8-row tile sweep on
  the prose prompt, and does that cost time (cardtile's card_in 23.05 against 17.76 ms a layer-batch)?
term: dram__bytes_read.sum, median over the full-block ds41_expert_gate_up_tiles launches of one
  prose prompt call (corpus-prose.ids, first 512), --place a, hot list 384, CED on, A6000
unit: GB
predict: 2.7..3.5
condition: just ncu-gpu-ds41-gt 512 with BLOOMERY_HOT_LIST=/root/bloomery-data/router/hotlist-384.txt
  under the lease, no other card work on the A6000; the summary must count at least 30 full-block GT
  launches and as many DT launches (one a MoE layer), or the grid filter picked the wrong set and the
  run is void; gpu__time_duration of both is read beside the bytes: the same-shape analogues give GT
  6.6..8.5 ms and DT 5.4..6.9 ms for a full layer, and the fit to card_in needs GT + DT near 16.8 ms
decide-in: bytes in band and GT past 8.5 ms: build the (expert, row tile, tile) block order in both
  tile kernels (bit for bit, S, 48-register budget as its gate), predicted card_in -1.2..-4.4 ms a
  layer-batch; bytes in band but GT inside 6.6..8.5 ms: the re-read is hidden, go as decide-below
decide-below: the activations stay in L2 (resident reads ~0.72 GB): the excess is per-block latency at
  5 blocks an SM; design gate and up in one walk (two rows a warp share each activation load) first
decide-above: both walks of a block miss (up to 6.0 GB): if GT is past 8.5 ms build the block order
  as in decide-in and price the up walk's second read into its gain, else as decide-below
